PromLight Universal @VERSION@
===============================

For macOS @MINIMUM_SYSTEM_VERSION@ or later on Apple silicon and Intel Macs.

IMPORTANT SECURITY NOTICE
-------------------------

This preview build is ad-hoc signed and has NOT been notarized by Apple.
macOS is therefore expected to block it on first launch and show a security
warning. Only continue if you received this package directly from someone you
trust and the checksum matches.

The checksum can detect a changed or corrupted download. It does not establish
the publisher's identity if the ZIP and checksum came from the same untrusted
source.

VERIFY THE DOWNLOAD (RECOMMENDED)
---------------------------------

Keep these two files together in the same folder:

  @ZIP_NAME@
  @CHECKSUM_NAME@

Open Terminal, change to that folder, and run:

  shasum -a 256 -c @CHECKSUM_NAME@

The expected result is:

  @ZIP_NAME@: OK

If the result is not OK, stop and request a fresh copy.

INSTALL
-------

1. Double-click @ZIP_NAME@ if your Mac did not unzip it automatically.
2. Open the extracted @RELEASE_NAME@ folder.
3. Drag PromLight.app into the Applications folder.
4. In Applications, double-click PromLight once.

FIRST LAUNCH: EXPECTED GATEKEEPER WARNING
-----------------------------------------

Because this preview is not Developer ID signed and notarized, macOS may say
that Apple cannot check it for malicious software or that the developer cannot
be verified. After attempting to open PromLight once:

1. Open the Apple menu and choose System Settings.
2. Select Privacy & Security and scroll down to the Security section.
3. Click Open Anyway for PromLight. The button is normally available for about
   one hour after the blocked launch attempt.
4. Authenticate if asked. When the warning appears again, click Open.

PromLight is then saved as an exception and should open normally in future.
On macOS Sequoia and later, do not rely on old Control-click instructions;
Apple requires this Privacy & Security flow. On a managed Mac, an administrator
may prevent the override.

If macOS says that PromLight "will damage your computer" or that the app is
damaged, stop. Do not bypass that warning. Delete the copy and request a fresh
package.

Apple's current guidance:
  https://support.apple.com/102445

NOTE FOR ZERO-WARNING DISTRIBUTION
----------------------------------

Ad-hoc signing is suitable for local testing, but it cannot provide a normal
zero-warning installation for another person. For that experience, the
distributor must sign PromLight with an Apple Developer ID Application
certificate, enable hardened runtime, submit it to Apple's notary service, and
staple the accepted notarization ticket before creating the final ZIP.

